    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d693">

                <pb facs="0744" n="732" sameAs="frus:frus1942v05/pg732"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">832.8595/4: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1942-03-27T00:00:00-04:00">March 27,
                        1942—midnight</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>807. Your 888, March 16, 7 p.m. Navy Department states that it has cabled the
                    Naval Attaché of your Embassy<anchor n="12" xml:id="d693-f1"/> to offer to the
                    Brazilian authorities all practicable assistance in routing Brazilian merchant
                    vessels en route to the United States.</p>
                <p>At present the British Naval Control Service is routing all Brazilian merchant
                    ships whose masters so request. However, the United States Navy is assigning
                    officers to all principal ports in the other American republics with
                    instructions to be prepared to assume full control of routing in this area about
                    July 1. In the meantime United States Naval Attachés and Observers in such ports
                    as they are stationed will be glad to render any available assistance of this
                    kind.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Welles</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d693-f1" n="12" xml:id="d693-f1-content">
                <p>Rear Adm. Augustin T. Beauregard.</p>
